<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>tech.kioh.Volar</string>
	<!--
	  CFBundleName / CFBundleDisplayName carry the marketing brand "Volar AI" (App Store listing,
	  under the Dock icon, About box). CFBundleExecutable below is deliberately left as `Volar`,
	  unrelated to branding — it is the actual binary filename on disk, and it is frozen alongside
	  CFBundleIdentifier (`tech.kioh.Volar`) for code-signing / path stability. Do not "fix" this
	  split by renaming the executable to match the display name.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Volar AI</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Volar AI</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>1.0.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>Volar</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<!--
	  LSUIElement was REMOVED 2026-07-26 (anh Khôi chốt). It used to be `true`, making Volar a pure
	  menu-bar agent: no Dock icon, no app menu, and no window at launch. Two problems with that:
	  (1) App Review opens the app, sees nothing happen, and rejects it as "app does not launch" —
	  the single most common rejection for menu-bar apps; (2) with no app menu, ⌘Q / ⌘W / ⌘, never
	  worked properly and a closed window could only be reopened from the menu bar.

	  Volar is now an ordinary app — Dock icon, app menu, Cmd-Tab — that ALSO keeps its MenuBarExtra.
	  Do NOT re-add this key without also re-reading `docs/app-store-submission-guide.md` §5.2.
	-->
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>14.0</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Fix (2026-07-27): both strings below used to promise the audio never leaves the device — true
	  when this app only had an on-device engine, but no longer true once Groq (cloud) became the
	  DEFAULT speech engine (see AppState.swift's speech-engine default). Rewritten to describe what
	  actually happens by default, without promising "unlimited" or "never leaves your device" —
	  see MicrophonePermission.swift and Speech/GroqEngine.swift for the engine this now describes.
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Volar listens while you hold the capture hotkey so it can turn what you say into a task. By default, audio is sent to Volar's cloud speech service to be transcribed; you can switch to fully on-device recognition in Settings.</string>
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>When you choose on-device recognition in Settings, Volar uses Apple's on-device speech recognizer to turn what you say into a task title, due date, priority, and duration — this recognition itself runs entirely on your Mac.</string>
	<!--
	  Calendar access — read AND write (updated: Volar now mirrors scheduled tasks into a calendar
	  it creates; it used to be read-only, this key's string changed accordingly, keep it truthful).
	  macOS 14+ has no read-only EventKit request API — the only call that grants read access is
	  `requestFullAccessToEvents()` (see CalendarAccess.swift's file header for the full
	  explanation), so this key is the one macOS shows in the TCC prompt for that call — and Volar
	  now genuinely uses both halves of that grant: it reads calendar metadata to see which time
	  blocks are free, and it writes into a calendar it creates itself, called "Volar", to mirror
	  scheduled tasks as events (opt-in, `CalendarSync.mirrorEnabled`, default off). Volar never
	  touches any calendar it did not create (`CalendarSync.swift`'s two ownership guards enforce
	  this in code, not just in this description). Calendar data stays on this Mac and is never
	  uploaded.
	-->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Volar reads your calendar to see which time blocks are genuinely free, and — if you turn on calendar mirroring in Settings — adds your scheduled tasks as events in a calendar it creates called "Volar". It only ever modifies events it created itself, never your other calendars, and nothing leaves your Mac.</string>
	<!--
	  Legacy key, kept alongside the one above purely as a fallback for any older EventKit code
	  path that still consults it instead of the macOS-14 key (Apple has not documented removing
	  the legacy key's relevance, and it costs nothing to keep it truthful and in sync). Same claim,
	  same behavior as the key above.
	-->
	<key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Volar reads your calendar to see which time blocks are genuinely free, and — if you turn on calendar mirroring in Settings — adds your scheduled tasks as events in a calendar it creates called "Volar". It only ever modifies events it created itself, never your other calendars, and nothing leaves your Mac.</string>
	<!--
	  feature 002 (contracts/app-links.md): registers the `volar://` app-link scheme, sandbox-safe
	  (no listening port). NOTE: this is declared directly in this physical Info.plist rather than
	  via xcodegen's `target.info.properties` mechanism, because this target already sets
	  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE: NO and points INFOPLIST_FILE straight at this file (see
	  project.yml) — xcodegen's `info:` block only applies when it is generating the Info.plist
	  itself, which is not this project's setup.

	  Handling: Shared/Orchestrator/AppLinkHandler.swift, called from `AppDelegate.application(_:open:)`.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>tech.kioh.Volar</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>volar</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Volar only ever talks HTTPS (Supabase Auth/Edge Functions, App Store/StoreKit) — no custom or
	  proprietary encryption. Declaring that here means every TestFlight/App Store Connect upload
	  skips the manual export-compliance questionnaire instead of asking every single time.
	-->
	<!--
	  Services menu — "New Task in Volar" on any text selection, system-wide (backlog "Đường vào
	  Volar" [I2]; docs/app-links.md already reserved this path as FR-040). `NSMessage` must match
	  `VolarServicesProvider.captureText(_:userData:error:)`'s selector name exactly, minus argument
	  labels — a mismatch produces a menu item that appears and does nothing.

	  `NSSendTypes` uses the legacy `NSStringPboardType` name deliberately: it is still what the
	  pasteboard server matches on for plain-text services, and is what Apple's own service samples
	  declare. `NSRequiredContext` left absent so the item offers itself on any text selection
	  rather than only inside text-editing contexts.

	  NOT VERIFIED and not verifiable off-Mac: a service only appears after the built app has been
	  seen by `pbs`. If it doesn't show up after a build+run, flush with
	  `/System/Library/CoreServices/pbs -flush` and relaunch the host app.
	-->
	<key>NSServices</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSMenuItem</key>
			<dict>
				<key>default</key>
				<string>New Task in Volar</string>
			</dict>
			<key>NSMessage</key>
			<string>captureText</string>
			<key>NSPortName</key>
			<string>Volar</string>
			<key>NSSendTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSStringPboardType</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
